ransition Metals in Olivine and Tavorite Cathode Materials for Li-Ion Batteries - PubMed - Density Functional Theory Investigation of Mixed Transition Metals in Olivine and Tavorite Cathode Materials for Li-Ion Batterie...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32186369/#1"},{"title":"Accurate Prediction of Voltage of Battery Electrode Materials Using Attention-Based Graph Neural Networks | Knihovny.cz - Accurate Prediction of Voltage of Battery Electrode Materials Using Attention-Based Graph Neural Networks","url":"https://www.knihovny.cz/SummonRecord/FETCH-LOGICAL-a436t-17b1a6527fee6852b1545362e214321636018af6dc24ad6b6629d3d05938e76c3?sid=25135312&lng=en#1"}]  ## Voltage calculations for tavorite LiMPO₄F (M = Mn, Fe, Co, Ni) cathodes  **Method.** First-principles DFT is the standard approach. Yu, Rosso, Zhang and Liu (*J. Mater. Chem.*, 2011, 21, 12054–12058) performed plane-wave DFT calculations on Li₂TMPO₄F, LiTMPO₄F and TMPO₄F (TM = V, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni) specifically to test their feasibility as high-voltage cathodes (>3.5 V vs Li metal); they computed structures, **average open-circuit voltages**, and thermal stabilities for step-wise lithiation/delithiation. For the related olivine LiMPO₄ series (M = Mn, Fe, Co, Ni), Le Bacq and Pasturel showed that **LDA+U** (not plain LDA) is required to reproduce experimental volumes and the experimental trend in intercalation voltage.  **Calculated voltages (Mueller et al., *Chem. Mater.* 2011, 23, 3854–3862, "Evaluation of Tavorite-Structured Cathode Materials").** Their Table 2 lists voltages relative to Li/Li⁺ for Li insertion into fluorophosphate hosts (each row gives successive insertion steps, plus volume change and specific energy):  - **Fe(PO₄)F**: 5.14 V, 2.92 V, 4.03 V; ~9% volume change; 1338 Wh/kg - **Mn(PO₄)F**: 4.86 V, 3.33 V, 4.15 V; 7–10% volume change; 1218 Wh/kg - **Ni(PO₄)F**: 5.50 V, 4.59 V, 5.04 V; 660 Wh/kg; 2216 Wh/L - **Mo(PO₄)F**: 3.33 V (other steps n.t.); 2% volume change; 411 Wh/kg (*Asterisks in the original mark cells where both end-members pass a stability test. Note: the extracted table snippet is garbled, so the exact redox couple assigned to each voltage — e.g. M²⁺/M³⁺ vs M³⁺/M⁴⁺ — is not fully resolvable from the snippet alone.)  **Stability screening.** Tavorite compounds predicted to release less than 30 meV/atom upon decomposition — and thus most likely to be directly synthesizable — include **LiCo(PO₄)F, LiCr(PO₄)F, LiFe(PO₄)F, LiMn(PO₄)F and Mo(PO₄)F**. Compounds releasing >0.1 eV/atom are judged unlikely to be stable enough for commercial use.  **Related fully-lithiated Li₂MPO₄F (edge-shared octahedra).** Li₂FePO₄F (orthorhombic Pbcn; a = 5.055 Å, b = 13.561 Å, c = 11.0526 Å, V = 858.62 Å³) has an OCV of ~3.0 V, lower than LiFePO₄ olivine (3.45 V). Li₂CoPO₄F and Li₂NiPO₄F have a theoretical capacity of ~310 mAh/g; the calculated intercalation voltage of **~4.9 V for Li₂CoPO₄F** agrees with the observed ~5 V plateau (with large first-cycle irreversibility attributed to electrolyte decomposition). The Li₂MPO₄F family shows small (~3.8%) volume change and simple 2-D Li⁺ pathways.  **Mixed transition metals.** DFT on Ni-substituted tavorite LiMn₀.₅Ni₀.₅PO₄F (LMNPF) gives an increased Li-intercalation voltage of **5.23 V** (olivine LMNP: 4.27 V), ~8% unit-cell volume expansion on lithiation (less than pristine MnPO₄F), and a Li migration barrier of 0.4 eV.  **Other/related values.** In the same chemistry family: fluorosulfates LiMn(SO₄)F, LiCo(SO₄)F, LiNi(SO₄)F calculate to 4.27 V, 4.93 V and 5.35 V; tavorite LiVPO₄F shows an 8.5% volume change with 655 Wh/kg. Machine-learning (attention-based graph neural network) models have also been used to predict average voltages, e.g. >3.1 V for NaₓMPO₄F (M = Ti, Cr, Fe, Cu, Mn, Co, Ni) frameworks.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
